We have 3 user roles

    1. Super Admin (ROLE_ADMIN) ->
        i) add, delete users
        ii) modify user scope
        iii) complete crud access
        iv) view logs
        v) All access of SRE

    2. Operator / SRE (ROLE_SRE)
        i) add, delete servers to monitor
        ii) configure collection interval
        iii) changing alert threshold
        iv) delete alerts

    3. Viewer (ROLE_VIEWER)
        i) view servers
        ii) view alerts
        iii) view thresholds

    4. Auditor (ROLE_AUDITOR) -> Optional
        i) view audit logs




Permissions :

Permission Name,Description
user:create,    Add new users to the system
user:delete,    Remove users from the system
user:modify,   Modify a user's roles/permissions
server:create,  Add new servers to monitor
server:read,    View monitored servers
server:delete,  Delete servers from monitoring
config:write,   Configure collection intervals and alert thresholds
config:read,    View configured alert thresholds
alert:read,     View triggered alerts
alert:delete,   Dismiss or delete alerts



DB Schema :
Users -> users have role(s) -> roles have permissions

users - [many to many] - roles -[many to many] - permissions.



Users -> users have role(s) -> roles have permissions
join tables : users_roles, roles_permissions
separate user details table for user meta data.

However, we might need to temporarily provide permissions to users which exceed their roles. so, we can create a new user_permissions table. This can map exceptional permissions to the user

So, users_permissions table for exceptional permissions.


users :
    user_id -> UUID
    username -> String
    password -> String -> hash
    email -> String -> Unique
    Status -> String Enum(ACTIVE/SUSPENDED)


user_details :
    user_id -> UUID -> from user table
    first_name -> String
    last_name -> String
    user_desc -> String
    created by -> UUID -> Creator user id
    created time -> datetime timestamp

Roles :
    role_id : int
    role_name : String
    description : String

Permissions :
    permission_id : int
    permission_name : String
    description : String

users_roles :
    user_id : UUID
    role_id :  Long
    assignment time : DATETIMEOFFSET

    -- Non-Clustered Index on role_id to optimize reverse lookups (e.g., "Find all users with Role X")
    CREATE NONCLUSTERED INDEX IX_USER_ROLES_MAPPING_RoleId
    ON USER_ROLES_MAPPING (role_id);

    Composite primary key with both ids

roles_permissions :
    role_id :  Long
    permission_id : Long
    Composite primary key with both ids

users_permissions :
    user_id : UUID
    permission_id : Long

    Composite primary key with both ids

